WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.640
- Measles now a public health emergency

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.640
with a surge of those infected.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.849
- Measles is back in the US.

00:00:06.849 --> 00:00:08.480
- The threat's so severe,

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.520
some hospitals arming each
entrance with security guards

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.110
to screen visitors for
symptoms of the virus.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:16.040
- In the first few months of 2019,

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.490
US doctors reported 314 cases of measles

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:21.710
to the Centers for Disease Control.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:23.770
Now compare that to the
number of reported cases

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.720
in the year 2000.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:26.943
Less than 100.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.153
What happened?

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.210
Scientists put much of the blame

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.050
on the anti-vaccine movement,

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.880
which is thriving in some
parts of the country.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.810
- Washington state has
been called a hotspot

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.200
because of its anti-vaccine movement.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.230
- Washington state, where more
than 70 cases were reported,

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.550
is one of 17 states that allows parents

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:48.700
to object to vaccinating their children

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.900
on the basis of personal or moral beliefs.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.940
And with fewer kids vaccinated,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.063
measles is far more likely to spread.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.320
In fact, the disease is so contagious

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:02.500
that 93 to 95% of a population

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.410
must be vaccinated to
prevent transmission.

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:07.270
Yet, in a number of states,

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.520
more parents are seeking
nonmedical exemptions

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.950
from routine immunizations.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.570
But here's the thing.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:15.890
Overall, the US is doing pretty well

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:17.990
when it comes to immunizations.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:20.240
If there is a big vaccine problem,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.420
it's not here in the US, but abroad

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.420
in Africa, in parts of Asia,

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.070
and in the Middle East.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:29.670
There were about 20
million infants in 2017

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.670
that didn't receive routine immunizations,

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.810
such as for diphtheria,
tetanus, and pertussis,

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.680
or DTP.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.453
And about 60% of them
live in 10 countries.

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.090
In Nigeria, for example,

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:43.810
only a third of the population

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:47.050
received the third dose of DTP in 2017,

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:49.150
while that rate is closer to 85%

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.100
for the rest of the world.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.610
You see, immunization rates
are tied not just to beliefs

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.530
but to economics.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:59.470
Poor countries often lack
infrastructure like electricity,

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:02.510
which is needed to keep
vaccines refrigerated.

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:04.580
Consider South Sudan.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.400
Less than 10% of the population

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.520
had access to electricity in 2016,

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.410
which might help explain why the country

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:15.130
has one of the lowest
immunization rates in the world.

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:17.603
But there are also other factors at play.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:19.793
Like conflict.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:23.460
Following the civil war
in Syria, for example,

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.320
immunization rates for some diseases

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.260
fell by nearly 40%,

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.700
and that trend is mirrored
by other countries

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.873
that are also marred
with political strife.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.160
Yes, these numbers look bleak,

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.080
but they are improving

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.580
thanks to an increase in awareness

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.780
and funding for immunization efforts,

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.560
especially in countries like Afghanistan,

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.870
Bangladesh, and Sudan.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:49.530
And in 2017,

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.900
a record 123 million
children were immunized

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.853
with at least one dose of DTP.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.480
So even as this anti-vaxxer movement

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.830
grips parts of the US,

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.870
as a global species,

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:04.560
humans are actually closer than ever

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.883
to ridding the world of
vaccine-preventable deaths.

